#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497942} {:answer/id #uuid "5245fbae-682c-5200-a3cc-b240ba6e18a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.490" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497995} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac017e5-f420-5574-9450-e76853f09d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 0.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498118} {:answer/id #uuid "9f618acf-fb41-51a6-99f4-2df360eb2078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pm 4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498156} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe37f3a-324e-5cb5-84ab-a56302e24787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 0.859" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497942} :answer-field/id #uuid "013e8c02-58d5-5e41-b09e-a87d19761e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c371072-128a-5610-adfb-0397399d02d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142491" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=80$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=80\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{80}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.438\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78caee98-945d-5e7f-a934-5470d31cd7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 1.789" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515124} {:answer/id #uuid "8d62915a-6993-5f3c-8131-308af074dc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pm 17.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515170} {:answer/id #uuid "c227e7cd-a039-5cfe-a0b7-7ab3d1a522da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 0.163" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515283} {:answer/id #uuid "d86d8d33-0f3d-5bb4-b955-814daea9d8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pm 1.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515328} {:answer/id #uuid "e09fe4af-a846-5ff7-a33c-6662acd0522b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 2.147" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78caee98-945d-5e7f-a934-5470d31cd7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 1.789" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515124} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbcdaec-a6e9-53b1-a803-289a0389e11e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43b89dfd-2b90-59a8-92b5-edcb02011dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-142493" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =10.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=3,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{120}} \\\\ 3\\pm 1.789\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.470\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497832} {:answer/id #uuid "15cb3f12-38ac-5ae3-b103-cecbd2328a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 3.527\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497850} {:answer/id #uuid "45c91c8a-9fcd-528c-80ed-69a7d1d14cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.008\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497957} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc6af7-ef67-5b4b-80fc-566d1be773ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 2.684\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498040} {:answer/id #uuid "a6df01ca-23cd-5023-bc1b-30fd13a5143d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.358\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.470\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497832} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a05532-9f78-5d65-a4d0-0f27f0b07a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb5fa69f-9c36-5007-b11d-0c6100a39ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143123" :question/problem "The amount of wine, in hectoliters, produced by one hectare of a vineyard in a particular region is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =2\\,hl.$ A sample of $120$ hectares is examined, and it is found that the mean volume of wine produced by each hectare is $15\\,hl.$\nFind a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean hectoliters per hectare $\\mu $ of the entire region.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=15\\,hl,\\,n=120,\\,\\sigma =2\\,hl.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{120}} \\\\ 15\\pm 0.470\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
